<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Proclamation of the German Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1871">1871</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The proclamation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> took place on 18 January 1871 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hall-of-mirrors">Hall of Mirrors</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a>, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-prussian-war">Franco-Prussian War</a>. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-i">Wilhelm I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> was proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-emperor">German Emperor</a>. The event, engineered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-von-bismarck">Otto von Bismarck</a>, marked the foundation of the German Empire as a federal state under Prussian leadership.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/proclamation-of-the-german-empire.jpg" alt="Proclamation of the German Empire" width="320" height="237" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anton von Werner · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_v_Werner_-_Kaiserproklamation_am_18_Januar_1871_(3._Fassung_1885).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Whether a united <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> would include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> had divided the German states since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a>. Otto von Bismarck, the Prussian minister president, held that unity was possible only without the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> monarchy. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-prussian-war">Austro-Prussian War</a> of 1866 dissolved the German Confederation of 1815, and after the victory at the Battle of Königgrätz Bismarck formed the North German Confederation without Austria, which adopted a constitution and became a state a year later.</p><p>The candidacy of Prince Leopold of Hohenzollern-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigmaringen">Sigmaringen</a> for the Spanish throne, backed by Bismarck, led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> to threaten war. Although the candidacy was withdrawn, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a> sent his ambassador Vincent Benedetti to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bad-ems">Bad Ems</a> to demand that King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-i">Wilhelm I</a> renounce any future claim, which Wilhelm refused. France declared war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> on 19 July 1870, and the southern German states joined Prussia under their defensive alliances.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Victories in August and September 1870 made the southern princes willing to join the North German Confederation. By the November Treaties, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a>, Hesse-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darmstadt">Darmstadt</a>, Württemberg and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> entered it on 1 January 1871, when a new constitution renamed the country the German Reich. On 9 and 10 December 1870 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a> had voted to offer the imperial title to the Prussian king. The date of 18 January recalled the coronation of Elector Frederick III of Brandenburg as Frederick I of Prussia in 1701.</p><p>German troops paraded round the palace while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> was under siege. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hall-of-mirrors">Hall of Mirrors</a>, whose ceiling glorified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> as a conqueror of German cities, regimental delegations raised their battle-torn banners. A service at a field altar ended with the hymn Nun danket alle Gott. Bismarck read out the proclamation, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-i-grand-duke-of-baden">Frederick I, Grand Duke of Baden</a>, Wilhelm's son-in-law, called out &quot;His Majesty, Kaiser Wilhelm&quot;, avoiding the constitutional title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-emperor">German Emperor</a>, which Wilhelm would not accept.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Several rulers, among them those of the Grand Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>, the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a> and Lippe, were not represented. The accounts of Wilhelm, Bismarck and the historian Albert von Pfister, present as a soldier, agree that a field altar stood in place of a throne, though they stress different aspects of the ceremony. These contemporary reports are considered more authentic than later portrayals, notably those in schoolbooks between 1918 and 1945, which were shaped by the defeat of the empire in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. The founding day of 18 January became a day of celebration.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Proclamation of the German Empire” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
